<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea</p><h1 class="enc-title">Talos</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Talos is a bronze giant of Greek mythology who guarded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a>, striding around the island three times a day and hurling stones at approaching ships. In the story of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argonauts">Argonauts</a> he was killed when Medea caused the single vein that held his life to be opened at the ankle. He appears on coins of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phaistos">Phaistos</a> and on painted vases, notably the Talos krater from Ruvo.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/talos.jpg" alt="Talos" width="320" height="408" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: ArchaiOptix · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Comparable_to_the_Group_of_Polygnotos_ARV_extra_-_death_of_Talos_-_draped_youths_02.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Talos is a man of bronze who protected <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piracy">pirates</a> and invaders, also spelled Talus or Talon. The sources vary widely on his role and genealogy. In the Argonautica of Apollonius of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhodes">Rhodes</a>, written in the first half of the 3rd century BCE, he is a descendant of the bronze race that sprang from ash-trees, bronze and invulnerable except for a vein in his ankle protected by only a thin layer of skin. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeus">Zeus</a> gave him to Europa to guard Crete, which he did by running around the island three times.</p><p>When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argo">Argo</a> approached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a> on its return voyage he kept it at bay by hurling boulders. Medea declared that she alone could defeat him, and by magic, summoning the keres, caused him to graze his ankle, so that the ichor drained from his body. Apollonius compares his fall to that of a felled pine tree, which has been taken to imply a size larger than human, though no ancient source states explicitly that he was a giant.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Pseudo-Apollodorus collected several traditions. Talos either belonged to the Race of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze">Bronze</a> or had been given to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minos">Minos</a> by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hephaestus">Hephaestus</a>, and he was either a bull or a bronze man. He guarded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a> by running round it three times daily and had a single vein from neck to ankles, stopped by a bronze nail. In one version Medea drove him mad with drugs, in another she promised immortality and pulled the nail, and in a third the Argonaut Poeas shot him in the ankle.</p><p>Pausanias reports from Cinaethon of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a> that he was the son of Cres, the personification of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a>, and from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ion-of-chios">Ion of Chios</a> that he was a son of Oenopion. Simonides told that Talos seized people of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a> who refused to go to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minos">King Minos</a> and leaped into flames holding them, a story given to explain the phrase sardonic laugh. The dialogue Minos, attributed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plato">Plato</a> in antiquity, rationalises him as a man who carried the laws of Crete, written on bronze tablets, round the villages three times a year.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Artistic sources linking Talos with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argonauts">Argonauts</a> predate Apollonius. Three kraters from around 400 BCE show his death. On the vase now in the Jatta National Archaeological Museum, the name vase of the Talos Painter, he falls backwards, painted white, into the arms of the Dioskouroi, while Medea holds a bowl and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poseidon">Poseidon</a> and Amphitrite look on.</p><p>A vase from Caudium and a fragment from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spina">Spina</a> show similar scenes, with a small winged figure, probably Thanatos, at his ankle. He also appears on three coins of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phaistos">Phaistos</a> of the Classical and early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hellenistic-period">Hellenistic periods</a>, as a naked winged figure throwing a stone, the wings read by Richard Buxton as a sign of his speed.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Talos” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>It minted coins from the 4th century BCE showing <b>Talos</b>, the bronze giant of myth, and Heracles.</blockquote><a href="/state/phaistos">Phaistos</a></li></ul></section>
